      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment. It's part 5 of 6 of a certificate markdown for OIML Certificate R49/2013-A-CZ1-24.02, Revision 3. The certificate covers GWF AG sonico NANO water meters.\n\nThe fragment contains several tables, each describing a model variant with:\n- Manufacturer: GWF AG\n- Model name: sonico NANO\n- Nominal diameter: 32 or 40\n- Q1, Q2, Q3, Q4 values\n- Q3/Q1 ratios\n\nLet me parse each table:\n\n**Table 1** (Nominal diameter 32, 6 variants):\n- Q1: 0.039, 0.063, 0.032, 0.050, 0.025, 0.040, 0.020, 0.032, 0.016, 0.025, 0.013, 0.020 (12 values, 2 per variant?)\n- Q2: 0.063, 0.100, 0.050, 0.080, 0.040, 0.064, 0.032, 0.051, 0.025, 0.040, 0.020, 0.032\n- Q3: 6.30, 10.00, 6.30, 10.00, 6.30, 10.00, 6.30, 10.00, 6.30, 10.00, 6.30, 10.00\n- Q4: 7.875, 12.50 (×6)\n- Q3/Q1: 160, 200, 250, 315, 400, 500 (each colspan=2)\n\nSo this is 6 variants with Q3/Q1 ratios of 160, 200, 250, 315, 400, 500. Each Q3/Q1 ratio corresponds to TWO values of Q1 (each colspan=2 in the Q3/Q1 row means 2 columns... wait, but there are 12 data cells and 6 ratio cells each spanning 2 columns).\n\nHmm, interesting. So each Q3/Q1 spans 2 data columns. But Q3 has 12 values (6.30, 10.00 repeated 6 times). So actually each Q3/Q1 ratio (e.g. 160) covers two variants: Q3=6.30 and Q3=10.00.\n\nLet me pair them:\n- Q3/Q1 = 160: Q3=6.30 → Q1=0.039, Q2=0.063, Q4=7.875; Q3=10.00 → Q1=0.063, Q2=0.100, Q4=12.50\n\nCheck: 6.30/0.039 ≈ 161.5, close to 160. 10.00/0.063 ≈ 158.7, close to 160. Yes.\n\nActually Q1 is usually computed as Q3/(Q3/Q1 ratio). 6.30/160 = 0.039375 ≈ 0.039. 10/160 = 0.0625 ≈ 0.063. Yes.\n\n- Q3/Q1 = 200: Q3=6.30 → Q1=0.032 (6.3/200=0.0315), Q2=0.050, Q4=7.875; Q3=10.00 → Q1=0.050, Q2=0.080, Q4=12.50\n- Q3/Q1 = 250: Q3=6.30 → Q1=0.025, Q2=0.040, Q4=7.875; Q3=10.00 → Q1=0.040, Q2=0.064, Q4=12.50\n- Q3/Q1 = 315: Q3=6.30 → Q1=0.020, Q2=0.032, Q4=7.875; Q3=10.00 → Q1=0.032, Q2=0.051, Q4=12.50\n- Q3/Q1 = 400: Q3=6.30 → Q1=0.016, Q2=0.025, Q4=7.875; Q3=10.00 → Q1=0.025, Q2=0.040, Q4=12.50\n- Q3/Q1 = 500: Q3=6.30 → Q1=0.013, Q2=0.020, Q4=7.875; Q3=10.00 → Q1=0.020, Q2=0.032, Q4=12.50\n\nSo Table 1 = DN32 with 12 variants: 6 ratios × 2 Q3 values.\n\n**Table 2** (Nominal diameter 32, 4 variants):\n- Q1: 0.010, 0.016, 0.013, 0.010\n- Q2: 0.016, 0.025, 0.020, 0.016\n- Q3: 6.30, 10.00, 10.00, 10.00\n- Q4: 7.875, 12.50, 12.50, 12.50\n- Q3/Q1: 630 (colspan 2), 800, 1000\n\nSo:\n- Q3/Q1 = 630: Q3=6.30 → Q1=0.010, Q2=0.016, Q4=7.875; Q3=10.00 → Q1=0.016, Q2=0.025, Q4=12.50\n- Q3/Q1 = 800: Q3=10.00 → Q1=0.013 (10/800=0.0125), Q2=0.020, Q4=12.50\n- Q3/Q1 = 1000: Q3=10.00 → Q1=0.010, Q2=0.016, Q4=12.50\n\n4 variants for DN32: ratios 630 (×2), 800, 1000.\n\nSo DN32 total: 16 variants.\n\n**Table 3** (Nominal diameter 40, 12 values):\n- Q1: 0.250, 0.400, 0.200, 0.320, 0.159, 0.254, 0.125, 0.200, 0.100, 0.160, 0.080, 0.128\n- Q2: 0.400, 0.640, 0.320, 0.512, 0.254, 0.406, 0.200, 0.320, 0.160, 0.256, 0.128, 0.205\n- Q3: 10.00, 16.0 (×6)\n- Q4: 12.50, 20.0 (×6)\n- Q3/Q1: 40, 50, 63, 80, 100, 125\n\nPairs:\n- 40: Q3=10 → Q1=0.250 (10/40=0.25), Q2=0.400, Q4=12.50; Q3=16 → Q1=0.400, Q2=0.640, Q4=20.0\n- 50: Q3=10 → Q1=0.200, Q2=0.320; Q3=16 → Q1=0.320, Q2=0.512\n- 63: Q3=10 → Q1=0.159, Q2=0.254; Q3=16 → Q1=0.254, Q2=0.406\n- 80: Q3=10 → Q1=0.125, Q2=0.200; Q3=16 → Q1=0.200, Q2=0.320\n- 100: Q3=10 → Q1=0.100, Q2=0.160; Q3=16 → Q1=0.160, Q2=0.256\n- 125: Q3=10 → Q1=0.080, Q2=0.128; Q3=16 → Q1=0.128, Q2=0.205\n\n12 variants for DN40 (first group).\n\n**Table 4** (Nominal diameter 40, 12 values):\n- Q1: 0.063, 0.100, 0.050, 0.080, 0.040, 0.064, 0.032, 0.051, 0.025, 0.040, 0.020, 0.032\n- Q2: 0.100, 0.160, 0.080, 0.128, 0.064, 0.102, 0.051, 0.081, 0.040, 0.064, 0.032, 0.051\n- Q3: 10.00, 16.0 (×6)\n- Q4: 12.50, 20.0 (×6)\n- Q3/Q1: 160, 200, 250, 315, 400, 500\n\nPairs:\n- 160: Q3=10 → Q1=0.063 (10/160=0.0625), Q2=0.100; Q3=16 → Q1=0.100, Q2=0.160\n- 200: Q3=10 → Q1=0.050, Q2=0.080; Q3=16 → Q1=0.080, Q2=0.128\n- 250: Q3=10 → Q1=0.040, Q2=0.064; Q3=16 → Q1=