nimal entry? The rule says \"Don't fabricate. If a field is not present in the markdown, omit it.\" I could include revision_history: [{\"revision\": \"3\"}] — but that's odd. I'll include it since it's clearly present data, with revision \"3\" and omit date/changes. Hmm. Actually let me think — revision_history is meant for a list of revisions. The fragment only says \"Revision 3\" as current revision. I think it's safer to include it as revision_history entry with revision \"3\" since that's the info present. Or maybe not include revision_history at all since it's a history table normally. I'll include a minimal entry: {\"revision\": \"3\"} — that conveys current revision is 3.\n\n- model_family: models for all variants. Since each table is identified only by nominal diameter and Q3/Q1 ratio and Q3 value, model IDs need to be constructed. E.g. \"DN32 Q3=6.3 R=160\" etc. The model_id is constructed by me — that's acceptable per the R60 example (\"Emax=25t\" was constructed).\n\n- characteristics.model_level: I could add attributes per model: nominal_diameter, q1, q2, q3, q4, q3_q1_ratio. That's 44 models × 6 attributes. That's big but doable. Alternatively use matrix_tables to preserve the tables and populate model_level.\n\nLet me structure:\n\nmodel_family:\n- family_name: \"sonico NANO\"\n- models: 44 entries:\n  - DN32:\n    - {\"model_id\": \"sonico NANO DN32 Q3=6.3 R160\", \"attributes\": {\"nominal_diameter\": 32, \"q3\": 6.3, \"q3_q1_ratio\": 160}}? Hmm, attributes at model level.\n\nActually, maybe simpler: model_id like \"DN32_Q3=6.3_Q3/Q1=160\". Let me define model_id format: \"sonico NANO DN32, Q3=6.3 m³/h, Q3/Q1=160\".\n\nLet me enumerate all 44 models:\n\nDN32 group A (Table 1, 12 models):\n1. Q3=6.3, R=160: Q1=0.039, Q2=0.063, Q4=7.875\n2. Q3=10, R=160: Q1=0.063, Q2=0.100, Q4=12.5\n3. Q3=6.3, R=200: Q1=0.032, Q2=0.050, Q4=7.875\n4. Q3=10, R=200: Q1=0.050, Q2=0.080, Q4=12.5\n5. Q3=6.3, R=250: Q1=0.025, Q2=0.040, Q4=7.875\n6. Q3=10, R=250: Q1=0.040, Q2