rived from Q3 and the ratio: Q1 = Q3/R (approximately, rounded per R49 OIML table), Q2 = 1.6·Q1, Q4 = 1.25·Q3. So the true axes are (DN, Q3, R). But the document presents all of Q1..Q4 and ratio, so I'll extract them all.\n\nModel ID: Let me use \"sonico NANO DN32 Q3=6.3 R160\" — hmm, \"R\" might be confused with recommendation. Maybe \"DN32/Q3=6.3/Ratio160\". Let me use format: \"sonico NANO DN32 Q3=6.3 Q3/Q1=160\". That's explicit.\n\nLet me create model_ids:\n- \"sonico NANO DN32 Q3=6.3 Q3/Q1=160\"\n- etc.\n\nAttributes in models[]: include nominal_diameter, q3, q3_q1_ratio maybe, but the full characteristics go in model_level. Actually, to avoid duplication, models[] entries can just have model_id, and characteristics.model_level holds the attribute×model matrix. In the R137 example, models[] only had model_id and model_level held the values. I'll follow that.\n\nmodel_level entries:\n1. {\"attribute\": \"nominal_diameter\", \"unit_symbol\": \"mm\"? — actually DN is dimensionless-ish; the table says \"Nominal diameter: 32\" without unit. DN32 conventionally means mm. But don't fabricate — no unit given. I could use unit_symbol: null. Hmm, DN is nominal diameter in mm typically. The table just says \"Nominal diameter: 32\". I'll use null unit or \"DN\"? Let me use unit_symbol: null to be safe. Actually maybe \"DN\" as the value prefix? Simpler: value 32, unit null.\n2. q1: unit m³/h, 44 values\n3. q2: unit m³/h, 44 values\n4. q3: unit m³/h, 44 values\n5. q4: unit m³/h, 44 values\n6. q3_q1_ratio: dimensionless, 44 values\n\nValues from rules: numbers not strings; 6.30 → 6.3; 10.00 → 10; 16.0 → 16; 12.50 → 12.5; 7.875 → 7.875; 20.0 → 20.\n\nMatrix tables: I'll create 5 entries, one per source table:\n\nTable 1: name \"sonico NANO DN32 flow characteristics (Q3/Q1 160–500)\"\ncolumns: [\"model\", \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q3\", \"q4\", \"q3_q1_ratio\"]\nrows: 12 rows as expanded.\n\nHmm wait — should I expand or preserve the colspan pairs? The instruction: \"preserve multi-dim tables\". The table as OCR'd has 12 value columns and 6 ratio cells (each spanning 2). Preserving means rows keyed by ratio: {\"q3_q1_ratio\": 160, \"q3\": [6.3, 10], \"q1\": [0.039, 0.063], ...}. That preserves the pairing ambiguity... Actually the pairing is unambiguous: column i pairs with ratio floor(i/2).\n\nI'll go with one row per variant for clarity, since model_level already has the full matrix and matrix_tables is a preservation mechanism. Each row: {\"model\": \"sonico NANO DN32 Q3=6.3 Q3/Q1=160\", \"q1\": 0.039, \"q2\": 0.063, \"q3\": 6.3, \"q4\": 7.875, \"q3_q1_ratio\": 160}.\n\ncolumn_units: [null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null].\n\nTable 2: DN32 Q3/Q1 630–1000, 4 rows.\nTable 3: DN40 Q3/Q1 40–125, 12 rows.\nTable 4: DN40 Q3/Q1 160–500, 12 rows.\nTable 5: DN40 Q3/Q1 630–1000, 4 rows.\n\nAlso \"Securing components and verification marks\" section: \"The sonico NANO meters have to be sealed by connecting the plastic seal on the plastic meter cover...\" Where to put this? There's no explicit schema field for sealing. Options: certified_type.description — the schema says \"description: free-text description if present\". This sealing text is type details. I could add it to certified_type.description. Hmm, but this fragment doesn't show a certified_type section explicitly... The fragment does contain \"Type details:\" tables, manufacturer, model name. I can build certified_type:\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Water meter\" (it's R49, water meters; sonico NANO is an ultrasonic water meter). The category list example includes \"Gas meter\", \"Measuring system\"... For R49 it's water meter. The fragment mentions \"water meter\" in the securing text: \"part of the body of the water meter\". So category \"Water meter\" is supported by text.\n- type_designations: [\"sonico NANO\"]\n- description: sealing description text.\n\nShould I include certified_type? The fragment has model name and manufacturer and sealing text. I think yes, minimal: category \"Water meter\", type_designations [\"sonico NANO\"], description with the sealing text.\n\ncertificate:\n- number: \"R49/2013-A-CZ1-24.02\"\n- scheme: \"A\" (from the \"-A-\" in number). Is that safe? R49/2013-A-CZ1-24.02 — yes, scheme A.\n- revision_history: [{\"revision\": \"3\"}] — hmm. The header says \"Revision 3\". Per schema, revision (string), date, changes. Only revision is known. I'll include {\"revision\": \"3\"} without fabricating date/changes. Actually... risk of noise. The instruction says omit sections not present; revision info IS present (Revision 3). I'll include it minimally.\n\nAlso \"Verze 20-001\" — that's the Czech \"Version 20-001\", likely a document/form version of the CMI template, page footer. Probably not certificate data; skip. Could be page_total: \"Page 8 of 10 pages\" → page_total: 10. The fragment shows pages 8, 9 (and page 10? No — last shown is page 9). \"Page 8 of 10 pages\" and \"Page 9 of 10 pages\". page_total = 10. Should I include? The schema has certificate.page_total. Yes, include 10.\n\nmanufacturer: GWF AG — manufacturers[]: [{\"name\": \"GWF AG\"}]. No address in fragment. Include name only.\n\nFootnotes: none in fragment.\n\nrecommendation: not stated in fragment (R49 implied by cert number but the fragment doesn't explicitly say \"R 49\"... the cert number \"R49/2013-A-CZ1-24.02\" strongly implies OIML R 49:2013. Should I extract recommendation {id: \"R49\", edition: 2013}? The cert number format for CMI: R49/2013-A-CZ1-24.02 → recommendation R49, edition 2013, scheme A, country CZ. Given rule \"Don't fabricate\" — this is a reasonable inference directly encoded in the number. The previous parts presumably had it, but I'm told to extract only from THIS fragment. I think including recommendation: {\"id\": \"R49\", \"edition\": 2013, \"scheme\": \"A\"} is justified from the certificate number itself. Hmm, borderline. The number literally contains \"R49/2013\". I'll include it — it's parsing the number, not fab